A1. An underworld broker. ~ FENCE
A5. How a young man feels after an engagement with a Follies Girl. ~ WISER
A9. This helps many a fisherman get along. ~ OAR
A10. These often give people the air. ~ FANS
A14. An insect unlike a wealthy scion. ~ ANT
A15. An article used indiscriminately without regard to sex. ~ AN
A17. Two Mussolinis might be called this. ~ DOMINATORS
A20. Where thoughtless women put their hats in the theater. ~ ON
A21. An adverb which expresses the effectiveness of prohibition. ~ NOT
A23. The kind of conditions that make for health. ~ SANITARY
A24. Never do this to a certain odoriferous quadruped of the feline species. ~ PAT
A25. This is never very good in a dry country. ~ CROP
A27. We do this to an openly "wet" senator. ~ ADMIRE
A28. What any old hen does. ~ SETS
A29. A person's part or share. ~ LOT
A31. A good thing to do for a due, due, due-y day. ~ SAVE
A32. Where the line of the least resistance forms. ~ BAR
A33. A race on the instalment plan. ~ RELAY
A35. A French article (not clothing). ~ LE
A36. Dams sometimes do this. ~ BURST
A38. What the person who gets up in the world does. ~ ARISES
A40. This comes between most Americans and a good drink. ~ BORDER
A41. There was many a wild knight in this in Arthur's day. ~ CASTLE
A42. What Hercules did with his Labors. ~ STROVE
A43. The first name of a member of a Royal theatrical family. ~ ETHEL
A44. Eleven hundred in Ancient Rome. ~ MC
A46. What a busy bee is continually buzzing about. ~ HONEY
A47. Where some big deals are put (poet.). ~ OER
A48. A juicy fruit. ~ PEAR
A50. A title of respect often used disrespectfully. ~ SIR
A51. What is an ace? ~ CARD
A53. A marine mammal. ~ WALRUS
A55. To give up. ~ CEDE
A58. A personal pronoun. ~ HIS
A59. A hard-boiled disciplinarian. ~ MARTINET
A61. How the older generation feels about the younger. ~ SAD
A62. What a cockney likes with his eggs. ~ AM
A63. These often have to be told where they get off. ~ PASSENGERS
A65. A supplementary part of some Spanish names. ~ DE
A66. A tree of the North. ~ FIR
A67. Unit of energy (plural). ~ ERGS
A68. An age never reached by the modern woman. ~ OLD
A70. An impure metallic product. ~ MATTE
A71. Fundamental unit of force (plural). ~ DYNES

D1. This doesn't amount to much any more in France. ~ FRANC
D2. Some people never take this for an answer. ~ NO
D3. A much despised man. ~ CAD
D4. Recipient of a Grecian maiden's prayer. ~ EROS
D5. What a chicken should be when it crosses the street. ~ WARY
D6. At out with the "outs." ~ INS
D7. Only an optimist tries to cross this (abbr.). ~ ST
D8. These are high on Broadway. ~ RENTS
D10. Discoveries in a gold-field. ~ FINDS
D11. The Kingdom Fundamentalists belong to. ~ ANIMAL
D12. An original settler. ~ NATIVE
D13. What a fully-dressed girl would cause you to do. ~ STARE
D16. A negative. ~ NOR
D18. Distress signal of the sisterhood of nannygoats. ~ MAA
D19. The miner's burden. ~ ORE
D20. Sometimes wild—in the plural. ~ OAT
D22. What you might call people who admit there's two sides to the argument. ~ TOLERATORS
D24. Anyone who does this can find an oasis in the great American desert—sh, speak easy. ~ PERSEVERES
D26. What you must be if you would shine. ~ POLISHED
D28. The humor of the critic. ~ SARDONIC
D30. The least-envied office of olden times. ~ TASTER
D32. These carry the gold-digger's compact. ~ BURROS
D33. It takes a speedy fellow to win this. ~ RACE
D34. One of the products of a touchdown. ~ YELL
D36. The two, taken by themselves. ~ BOTH
D37. When 3's are wild you want this. ~ TREY
D39. Where a hunter goes upon sighting a grizzly in the Northwest (abbr.). ~ SE
D40. A degree (init.). ~ BS
D44. A foundryman. ~ MELTER
D45. What a lover hopes his girl is doing. ~ CARING
D48. Very few Judges attempt to do this to their sentences. ~ PARSE
D49. There's a lot of these on the ladder to success. ~ RUNGS
D51. A big ditch. ~ CHASM
D52. What you must have to get along in Chicago. ~ AIM
D53. Has been. ~ WAS
D54. What few can do at a championship fight. ~ SEE
D56. The old block. ~ DAD
D57. What Californian gardens are—to Californians. ~ EDENS
D59. According to a song, she ain't what she used to be. ~ MARE
D60. What the Germans did when they goose-stepped. ~ TROD
D63. Joseph's downfall. ~ PIT
D64. Usual characteristic of an old rascal. ~ SLY
D66. The dogs of Charleston (abbr.). ~ FT
D69. These two letters, separated by — —, means a cuss word. ~ DN


